#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Zero-noise extrapolation with probabilistic error amplification for the "
"latest version of Qiskit mitigation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Zero-noise extrapolation with probabilistic error amplification"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<OpenInLabBanner notebookPath=\"addons/qiskit-mitigation/guides/pea-with-"
"noise-learning.ipynb\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) is a technique for mitigating quantum noise "
"in observable expectation value calculations. It works by executing a "
"quantum circuit at increasing noise scales, taking expectation values with "
"respect to those noise scales, and extrapolating to estimate a zero-noise "
"expectation value. Both the noise amplification and extrapolation stages can"
" be implemented in different ways. In this guide you will amplify the noise "
"with probabilistic error amplification (PEA), as suggested in [\"Evidence "
"for the utility of quantum computing before fault "
"tolerance\"](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06096-3). To amplify"
" with PEA, you must first model the noise affecting the circuit's entangling"
" gates.  You can also implement amplification with digital gate folding, "
"which needs no noise model and is covered in the [gate folding "
"guide](/addons/qiskit-mitigation/guides/gate-folding-with-custom-"
"extrapolation). You will learn the noise with [``qiskit-noise-"
"learning``](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-noise-learning) and extrapolate"
" with the built-in exponential fitting strategy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Keep in mind, while ZNE can be effective at mitigating quantum noise for a "
"lower sampling cost than other methods, it comes with no bounds on the "
"bias.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![ZNE extrapolation](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-"
"mitigation/zne_extrapolation.svg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The workflow is as follows:\n"
"1. Specify a circuit and observable\n"
"2. Compute exact expectation values using Qiskit's statevector simulator\n"
"3. Compute noisy, unmitigated expectation values\n"
"4. Learn the noise of the entangling layers with ``qiskit-noise-learning``\n"
"5. Prepare a ZNE program with probabilistic error amplification and sample circuits of varying noise scales\n"
"6. Extrapolate to the zero-noise limit\n"
"7. Compare the mitigated expectation values\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Specify a circuit and observable {#specify-a-circuit-and-observable}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this guide you will calculate the site-averaged magnetization $\\langle Z"
" \\rangle_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\langle Z_i "
"\\rangle$ of a Trotterized transverse-field Ising model (TFIM) on eight "
"qubits in a ring topology. You will sweep several values of the strength of "
"the transverse field and plot the magnetization as a function of the field "
"strength. In a ring topology on an even number of qubits, the two-qubit gate"
" layers can be split into two unique layers, as the red and green colors "
"show in the coupling map below. Designing circuits to have a minimal set of "
"unique entangling layers is important if you want to learn a noise model, "
"because the unique layers must be learned independently. If you have too "
"many unique layers in your circuit, noise learning can become infeasible due"
" to the sampling cost of learning many layers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-"
"mitigation/guides/pea-with-noise-learning/extracted-"
"outputs/2bf3d4ef-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
